{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lm5jxaS7NJorUEPqbBiPLS","type":"tool_result","content":"18:- **Reaper** — marks dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`, keeps their evidence 24 h, then cleans up\n37:        X[\"Reaper: ard:executor:failed, keep 24 h\"]\n80:| `ard:executor:failed` | Reaper or close-out | Job died or ran over time; resources kept 24 h |\n251:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode \n286:## Executors and the Reaper\n290:Local-executor details: the role directory (and its `Dockerfile`) is mounted from the config snapshot at the job's SHA, not from the working tree. The job worktree is always detached at a SHA resolved from the job's ref (the executor never checks out a branch; the committer owns branch worktrees\n292:A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n294:**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting\n307:**Controller.** `Prepare` builds the local workspace. It mints the read token, the key and the per-job Secret (owned by the Pod), and revokes the token (`DELETE /installation/token`) after `pre` terminates; tokens join the redaction set. Completion is decided only from the kubelet (`containerSta\n311:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --stric\n315:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged \n317:**Usage is recorded for every job, whatever its outcome.** The Finisher stores each agent step's usage (the models it reported, the four token counts, input, output, cache write and cache read, and the cost) in local state, keyed by job and executor step id, so a replay or a Reaper re-parse neve\n319:**The Reaper runs every minute and decides failure, not the agent.** It marks a job failed when the process or pod is gone without a close-out, the deadline passes (it kills the job first), the heartbeat goes quiet for 10 minutes, or the job is `:running` with no record in local state. Every fai\n321:1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent\n323:3. **Retention.** The worktree and job home are kept 24 hours after the job ended, then removed, together with the host logs of a failed job (the raw combined log is already deleted at close-out once its redacted copy is committed). A human extends this by adding `ard:keep` to the issue (or PR, \n326:Host log files older than 7 days are pruned hourly. The Reaper keeps running while the controller is paused.\n338:3. **Steps.** One worker job is one state row, one pool slot and one attempt, but it runs several executor jobs, each in a fresh worktree detached at a SHA: the agent step (`<job id>`) at the branch head, or `origin/main` for a new branch; a test step (`<job id>t<k>`) and a fix step (`<job id>f<\n344:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request n\n361:6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the reque\n443:- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess (or, with `ARD_AGENT_IMAGE` set, a role that reads untrusted input runs in that image) and under `executor: k8s` every role runs the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters) and a r\n452:#tokens_per_day: 40M        # optional per-role daily token default (40M, 1.5M, 500k or a plain integer); a role may set its own; unset = no token cap; recorded only, not enforced yet\n488:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" \n518:- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage`\n520:- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowerc