{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNXuxYH2QuhaeKAHfoyd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Rrb4S3is9ci3b6jbEcCfVK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 518p docs/proposal.md; sed -n 294p docs/proposal.md | cut -c1-1500; sed -n 321p docs/proposal.md | cut -c1-900; grep -n \"nothing is enforced\\|tokens_per_day\" docs/proposal.md | cut -c1-120; ls docs; grep -rn \"proposal.md\" --include=*_test.go . | head -3"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2514,"cache_read_input_tokens":36782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2514},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9855999e-70a3-42e7-926d-e47c97789325","uuid":"fbcb5982-3623-4e28-92a0-242e3e7ec73a","timestamp":"2026-11-24T22:22:13.705Z","request_id":"req_011ChNXuxQbkmt2jSb1dwRGw"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rrb4S3is9ci3b6jbEcCfVK","type":"tool_result","content":"- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are also recorded per role (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap; nothing is enforced yet). Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting o\n1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\n452:#tokens_per_day: 40M        # optional per-role daily token default (40M, 1.5M, 500k or a plain integer); a role may\n488:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. I\n518:- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have \nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\nsite\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*_test.go","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9855999e-70a3-42e7-926d-e47c97789325","uuid":"a31760da-811f-480b-9cbb-2cc6eb4a9791","timestamp":"2026-11-24T22:22:13.755Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are also recorded per role (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap; nothing is enforced yet). Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running j